==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|lacquey|lacky}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|laquais}}, which is probably (via {{etyl|pro|en}} {{m|pro|lacai}}?) from {{etyl|es|en}} {{m|es|lacayo}}, itself perhaps from {{etyl|it|en}} {{m|it|lacchè}} and {{etyl|el|en}} {{m|el|λακές}}, from {{etyl|tr|en}} {{m|tr|ulak}}. Another possibility is through {{etyl|fr|en}}, from {{etyl|ca|en}} {{m|ca|alacay}}, from {{etyl|ar|en}} {{m|ar|قَاضٍ|اَلْقَاضِي|magistrate}}. See French {{m|fr|laquais}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈlæ.ki/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|æki|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[footman]], a [[livery|liveried]] male servant.
#* {{quote-book|author=John Ruskin|authorlink=John Ruskin|chapter=By the Rivers of Waters|title=“Our Fathers Have Told Us.”: Sketches of the History of Christendom for Boys and Girls who have been Held at Its Fonts|volume_plain=part I (The Bible of Amiens)|location=Orpington, Kent|publisher=[[w:George Allen (publisher)|George Allen]]|year=1884|pages=30–31|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/ourfathershaveto00rusk#page/30/mode/2up|oclc=222616845|passage=St. [[w:Martin of Tours|Martin [of Tours]]] looks round, first, deliberately;—becomes aware of a tatterdemalion and thirsty-looking soul of a beggar at his chair side, who has managed to get ''his'' cup filled somehow, also—by a charitable '''lacquey'''. St. Martin turns his back on the Empress, and hobnobs with ''him''!}}
# A [[fawn|fawning]], servile follower; a [[lickspittle]].

